---
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import InstallCommand from "../../components/InstallCommand.astro";
import PluginReference from "../../components/PluginReference.astro";
import SocialcardSample from "../../components/SocialcardSample.astro";
import { getPlugin } from "../../data/plugins";
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";

const plugin = getPlugin("socialcard")!;

const tocEntries = [
  { id: "what-it-does", text: "What it does", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "install", text: "Install", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "register", text: "Register", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "configuration", text: "Configuration", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "props", text: "Props", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "templates", text: "Templates", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "using-the-card", text: "Using the card in your layout", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "no-component", text: "No component", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "for-agents", text: "For agents", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
];
---

<Doc
  tocEntries={tocEntries}
  currentSlug="socialcard"
  title="socialcard"
  section="@plugdash/socialcard"
  description="Open Graph share images for EmDash posts, generated on publish."
>
  <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    On publish, socialcard draws a 1200x630 PNG card with the post's
    title, author and date, uploads it to your media library as
    <code>og-&lt;id&gt;.png</code>, and sets it as the post's SEO image
    (<code>seo.image</code>). That SEO update goes live at once; it does
    not create a draft. Templates that use <code>getSeoMeta()</code> and
    <code>EmDashHead</code>, like the official blog template, then write
    <code>og:image</code> and <code>twitter:image</code> with no changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The work runs after the publish response, so editors do not wait.
    Republishing an unchanged post does nothing. When a card is redrawn,
    the old media item is deleted. A rendering or upload error is logged,
    never thrown.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <InstallCommand command={plugin.installCommand} />

  <h2 id="register">Register</h2>
  <p>It is a native plugin, so it goes in <code>plugins</code>, not <code>sandboxed</code>.</p>
  <Code language="js" filename="astro.config.mjs" code={`import { defineConfig } from "astro/config"
import emdash from "emdash/astro"
import { socialcardPlugin } from "@plugdash/socialcard"

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    emdash({
      plugins: [
        socialcardPlugin({
          template: "bold",
          background: "#111827",
          foreground: "#fef3c7",
        }),
      ],
    }),
  ],
})`} />

  <h2 id="configuration">Configuration</h2>
  <p>
    Options are passed to the plugin at runtime, in dev and in production.
    Changing any option gives every post a new card on its next publish.
    An SEO image set by hand in the admin is kept unless you pass
    <code>overwriteSeoImage: true</code>.
  </p>
  <PluginReference plugin={plugin} />

  <h2 id="templates">Templates</h2>
  <p>There are three built-in layouts:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>default</strong> - large title, author and date byline, subtle gradient</li>
    <li><strong>minimal</strong> - title only, on a clean paper background</li>
    <li><strong>bold</strong> - large type against a strong colour block</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Here's the same post rendered with each one:</p>
  <SocialcardSample />
  <p>
    Titles over 80 characters are cut with an ellipsis. The author comes
    from a <code>data.author</code> field (string or <code>&#123; name &#125;</code>),
    then the post's primary byline. A missing author or date drops that
    half of the byline. The bundled font is Lexend, Latin subset; titles in
    other scripts need a font passed with <code>fontFiles</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="using-the-card">Using the card in your layout</h2>
  <p>
    If your layout uses <code>getSeoMeta()</code> and
    <code>EmDashHead</code>, there is nothing to do. Otherwise read
    <code>post.seo.image</code> and prefix it with the request origin:
  </p>
  <Code language="astro" code={`---
const image = post.seo?.image
const ogImage = image ? new URL(image, Astro.url.origin).href : undefined
---

{ogImage && <meta property="og:image" content={ogImage} />}
{ogImage && <meta name="twitter:image" content={ogImage} />}`} />

  <h2 id="no-component">No component</h2>
  <p>
    socialcard has no companion Astro component. The card reaches the
    page through your existing SEO tags.
  </p>

  <h2 id="for-agents">For agents</h2>
  <p>
    After installing and registering <code>socialcardPlugin()</code>,
    publish a post and check that its page has an <code>og:image</code>
    tag pointing at a URL that returns <code>image/png</code>. If not:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Posts published before install get a card on their next publish.</li>
    <li>Check the layout writes <code>og:image</code> from <code>getSeoMeta()</code> or <code>post.seo.image</code>.</li>
    <li>The collection must have SEO support; others are skipped with one log line.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Data stored: a PNG in the media library, the post's <code>seo.image</code>, and a hash in plugin KV.</p>
</Doc>
